```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>"
)
```

```{r setup}
library(mixtureEM)
```

Some research questions are about two typologies at once. Do the profiles
of childhood adversity a survey finds go with the profiles of substance use
it also finds? Is that link the same for girls and boys? Each typology is a
latent class variable measured by its own items, and the question is about
the *association* between the two. `fit_joint_classes()` fits both latent
variables in one model, with the association written as a multinomial
logistic regression of one on the other, the loglinear model with latent
variables of Hagenaars (1993). Covariates can predict either variable, and
a covariate's effect on the second can differ across the classes of the
first, which is how a moderated association is written.

What a latent class analysis is, and how to choose the number of classes,
is in `vignette("class_enumeration")`; here each variable's number of
classes is taken as settled.

## The data

The example is simulated, so that the answer is known. For 1,500
adolescents there are six yes/no items about adverse childhood
experiences (`a1` to `a6`) and five items about substance use, each
answered never (1), sometimes (2) or often (3) (`s1` to `s5`). Behind
them are two latent variables with three classes each: adversity (`ca`:
low, household adversity, high) and substance use (`cs`: abstains, drinks,
several substances). High adversity goes with several substances, and
being a girl lowers the odds of several substances, most of all after high
adversity. Three answers in a hundred are missing at random.

```{r data}
set.seed(7070)
n <- 1500
female <- rbinom(n, 1, 0.5)
# Adversity class: 1 low, 2 household, 3 high; girls a little more often 2.
lp <- cbind(0, -0.6 + 0.5 * female, -1.2)
ca <- apply(exp(lp) / rowSums(exp(lp)), 1, function(p) sample(1:3, 1, prob = p))
# Substance-use class given adversity: 1 abstains, 2 drinks, 3 several
# substances. Logits against class 1, by adversity class; being female lowers
# the odds of several substances, most of all after high adversity.
assoc <- rbind(c(0, -0.4, -2.2), c(0, 0.3, -0.6), c(0, 0.6, 0.9))
lc <- assoc[ca, ] + cbind(0, 0, -c(0.2, 0.4, 1.2)[ca] * female)
cs <- apply(exp(lc) / rowSums(exp(lc)), 1, function(p) sample(1:3, 1, prob = p))
# Six yes/no adversity items and five never/sometimes/often use items.
p_a <- rbind(c(.10, .08, .12, .05, .10, .06),
             c(.70, .65, .55, .20, .15, .10),
             c(.80, .75, .70, .65, .60, .55))
adversity <- matrix(rbinom(n * 6, 1, p_a[ca, ]), n, 6,
                    dimnames = list(NULL, paste0("a", 1:6)))
use_level <- rbind(c(0, 0, 0, 0, 0), c(2, 0, 1, 0, 0), c(2, 2, 2, 1, 1))
use <- matrix(NA_integer_, n, 5, dimnames = list(NULL, paste0("s", 1:5)))
for (j in 1:5) {
  hi <- use_level[cs, j]
  r <- runif(n)
  use[, j] <- 1 + ifelse(hi == 0, (r > .80) + (r > .95),
              ifelse(hi == 1, (r > .30) + (r > .80), (r > .10) + (r > .40)))
}
adversity[runif(n * 6) < 0.03] <- NA
use[runif(n * 5) < 0.03] <- NA
```

`ca` and `cs` are the truth the analysis will try to recover; a real
analysis never has them. All it has is `adversity`, `use` and `female`.

## One step or two

The model can be estimated in one step, every parameter at once, or in two
(Bakk & Kuha, 2018). In two steps, step 1 fits each latent variable's
measurement model on its own, as two separate latent class analyses, and
step 2 holds those item parameters fixed while it estimates the association
and the covariate effects. The two-step estimator has a property that
matters when several structural models are compared: the classes are
defined by their own items only, so they mean the same thing in every
model, and a covariate added in step 2 cannot change what a class is. Its
standard errors include the uncertainty of step 1. The analysis below is
two-step, with the one-step fit as a check at the end.

Every fit switches off the package's default priors, which keep estimates
away from the boundary and are the right default for exploratory work.
Without them the fits are plain maximum likelihood, the estimator the
two-step method and the likelihood-ratio tests below are defined for;
`vignette("estimation")` explains what the priors add and when to keep
them.

```{r ml}
ml <- list(smoothing = 0, bayes_constants = list(categorical = 0))
```

## The association with covariates

The first structural model has the association and `female` predicting
both variables, its effect on `cs` the same in every adversity class
(`cs_effects = "common"`, the default).

```{r m1, cache = TRUE}
fem <- data.frame(female = female)
m1 <- fit_joint_classes(adversity, use, n_ca = 3, n_cs = 3,
                        measurement_cs = "ordinal",
                        predictors_ca = fem, predictors_cs = fem,
                        n_steps = 2, n_init = 20, random_state = 1,
                        smoothing = ml$smoothing,
                        bayes_constants = ml$bayes_constants)
```

Before the association, what the classes are. `measurement_summary()`
gives each variable's item profiles under its own classes, beside the
observed share of each answer:

```{r profiles}
measurement_summary(m1)
```

The adversity classes are the three that were simulated: `CA#1` reports
almost none of the six experiences, `CA#2` the first three (household
adversity) and few of the rest, `CA#3` all six at around one half or more.
The substance-use classes are too: `CS#1` mostly answers never, `CS#2`
drinks often (`s1`) and sometimes uses the third substance, and `CS#3`
uses the first three substances often and the last two sometimes.

Four probabilities have run to 0 or 1, and the note under the tables says
how to read them. The household class never reporting `a6` is a finding
about that class rather than a fault of the fit; it was simulated at 0.10,
and in a class of a few hundred the estimate of a rare answer can be zero.

Now the association:

```{r m1-print}
m1
```

Every log-odds is against the last class of each variable: `CS#1 ON CA#1`
of 2.40 says that low adversity raises the odds of abstaining, rather than
using several substances, by a factor of `exp(2.40) = 11` compared with
high adversity. The conditional probabilities say the same thing on the
probability scale: 4% of the low-adversity class use several substances,
22% of the high-adversity class.

## Is there an association at all?

The model with `association = "none"` holds the two variables independent
given the covariates. Fitted on the same step 1 (`step1 = m1`), it is the
restricted model of a likelihood-ratio test of the association, on
(3 - 1) x (3 - 1) = 4 degrees of freedom.

```{r m0, cache = TRUE}
m0 <- fit_joint_classes(adversity, use, n_ca = 3, n_cs = 3,
                        measurement_cs = "ordinal",
                        predictors_ca = fem, predictors_cs = fem,
                        association = "none", n_steps = 2, step1 = m1,
                        smoothing = ml$smoothing,
                        bayes_constants = ml$bayes_constants)
lr_test(m0, m1)
```

The two typologies are clearly associated.

## Is the association the same for girls and boys?

`cs_effects = c(female = "by_ca")` lets the effect of `female` on
substance use differ by adversity class. Against `m1`, that is a test of
whether sex moderates the association, again on four degrees of freedom:
one more slope per `cs` logit for each adversity class but the last.

```{r m2, cache = TRUE}
m2 <- fit_joint_classes(adversity, use, n_ca = 3, n_cs = 3,
                        measurement_cs = "ordinal",
                        predictors_ca = fem, predictors_cs = fem,
                        cs_effects = c(female = "by_ca"),
                        n_steps = 2, step1 = m1,
                        smoothing = ml$smoothing,
                        bayes_constants = ml$bayes_constants)
lr_test(m1, m2)
```

The moderation is significant at the 5% level, if not by much. The
coefficients of a moderated model are hard to read directly (each
`female | CA#k` row is a difference from the last adversity class), so
the result is best reported as the probabilities of each substance-use
class given each adversity class, for boys and for girls.
`joint_probabilities()` computes them at fixed covariate values, with
delta-method standard errors:

```{r probabilities}
boys  <- joint_probabilities(m2, at = list(female = 0))
girls <- joint_probabilities(m2, at = list(female = 1))
cbind(boys[, c("ca", "cs")], boys = round(boys$estimate, 3),
      girls = round(girls$estimate, 3))
```

After high adversity (`CA#3`), 32% of boys are in the several-substances
class and 9% of girls; after low adversity (`CA#1`) both are under 5%. That
is the moderation that was simulated: the link from adversity to using
several substances is much stronger for boys.

Any other function of the coefficients, with its standard error, is
available from `derived_estimates()`, using the parameter names
`joint_coefficients()` documents.

## The one-step fit as a check

The same three models in one step, every parameter estimated at once:

```{r onestep, cache = TRUE}
one <- lapply(list(m0 = "none", m1 = "common", m2 = "by_ca"), function(s)
  fit_joint_classes(adversity, use, n_ca = 3, n_cs = 3,
                    measurement_cs = "ordinal",
                    predictors_ca = fem, predictors_cs = fem,
                    association = if (s == "none") "none" else "free",
                    cs_effects = if (s == "by_ca") c(female = "by_ca") else "common",
                    n_init = 20, random_state = 1, standard_errors = FALSE,
                    smoothing = ml$smoothing,
                    bayes_constants = ml$bayes_constants))
lr_test(one$m0, one$m1)
lr_test(one$m1, one$m2)
```

The conclusions do not change: a strong association, and a moderation by
sex that is significant at the 5% level. The one-step log-likelihoods are
higher, as they must be, since step 1's item parameters are free there; the
price is that the classes' meaning can shift a little from one structural
model to the next.

## Summary

- `fit_joint_classes()` fits two latent class variables and their
  association in one model; `measurement_summary()` shows what each
  variable's classes are.
- `association = "none"` and `cs_effects = "by_ca"` give the restricted and
  the extended models of the two natural tests, run by `lr_test()`.
- With `n_steps = 2`, fit step 1 once and hand it to every structural
  model with `step1 =`, so that all of them share one measurement model.
- Report a moderated association as conditional probabilities at chosen
  covariate values, with `joint_probabilities()`.

## References

Bakk, Z., & Kuha, J. (2018). Two-step estimation of models between latent
classes and external variables. *Psychometrika*, 83(4), 871-892.

Hagenaars, J. A. (1993). *Loglinear models with latent variables*. Sage.
